From: ROCKNIX
Subject: [PATCH] cfg80211: indicate a disconnect once, not once per path

003 fixed the double release on the shutdown path, but the same fault is
reachable from an ordinary userspace disconnect, with no poweroff involved.
`nmcli device disconnect wlan1` is enough to take the board down:

  Internal error: Oops: 0000000096000021 [#1] SMP
  RTW: netdev_vir_if_close(wlan1) , bup=1
  Workqueue: cfg80211 cfg80211_event_work
  pc : cfg80211_unhold_bss+0x2c/0xa0
  lr : cfg80211_wdev_release_bsses+0x38/0xc0
  x19: 0000000000000000

x19 is the BSS being released and it is NULL, because a previous release
already cleared it.

The driver tells cfg80211 about a disconnect from more than one place. A
single `nmcli device disconnect` produces two indications, roughly a tenth
of a second apart -- one from _rtw_disconnect() under cfg80211_rtw_disconnect(),
and one from the netdev_close() that follows when the interface is taken
down:

  RTW: rtw_cfg80211_indicate_disconnect(wlan0) call cfg80211_disconnected, reason:0
  RTW: netdev_close(wlan0) , bup=1
  RTW: rtw_cfg80211_indicate_disconnect(wlan0) call cfg80211_disconnected, reason:0
  RTW: Free disconnecting network of scanned_queue failed due to pwlan == NULL

__cfg80211_disconnected() does not defend itself. It calls
cfg80211_wdev_release_bsses() unconditionally, having only checked the
iftype, so the second indication releases BSSes the first one already put
and cfg80211_put_bss() faults. The pwlan == NULL line above is the driver
noticing the same thing one layer up.

So the indication has to be idempotent, and the natural test is whether
cfg80211 still believes it is connected. wdev->connected is what
__cfg80211_disconnected() clears, so checking it lets whichever path gets
there first do the teardown and makes the rest no-ops.

An earlier version of this patch instead set
RTW_CFG80211_BLOCK_DISCON_WHEN_DISCONNECT so that cfg80211_rtw_disconnect()
suppressed the indication for the duration of _rtw_disconnect(). That
stopped the oops, but it silenced the wrong one of the two: the suppression
is lifted before netdev_close() runs, so the surviving indication was the
one from interface-down. That is fine when the interface does go down, but
a plain disconnect that leaves it up produced no indication at all, and
wdev->connected stayed set on an interface that was already down with
NO-CARRIER. iw dev wlan0 info still printed an ssid line for it.

That is not cosmetic, because nl80211_check_scan_flags() refuses a scan
carrying NL80211_SCAN_FLAG_RANDOM_ADDR while the wdev is connected:

	if (!(wiphy->features & randomness_flag) ||
	    (wdev && wdev->connected))
		return -EOPNOTSUPP;

iwd randomizes scans when it is not associated, so every scan after such a
disconnect came back -EOPNOTSUPP, it never got a fresh BSS list, and it
could not associate again until it was restarted. NetworkManager reports
that as "Secrets were required, but not provided", which points at the PSK
and wastes a lot of time.

The second hunk is unrelated to the count and was in the original patch.
valid_links is the MLO link bitmap and is 0 for an ordinary non-MLO
connection, which still uses links[0]. Gating the current_bss teardown on
it means the branch never runs, so rtw_wdev_unregister() leaves current_bss
set on a wdev that is going away -- the pre-5.19 code it replaced
(`if (wdev->current_bss)`) had no such condition.

Confirmed on a Miyoo Flip running 7.0.2: disconnect and reconnect cycles
complete, wdev->connected clears, randomized scans are accepted again, and
suspend/resume no longer trips the release.

--- a/os_dep/linux/ioctl_cfg80211.c
+++ b/os_dep/linux/ioctl_cfg80211.c
@@ -1041,6 +1041,16 @@
 			rtw_cfg80211_connect_result(pwdev, NULL, NULL, 0, NULL, 0,
 				reason?reason:WLAN_STATUS_UNSPECIFIED_FAILURE,
 				GFP_ATOMIC);
+#if (LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(5, 19, 2))
+		} else if (!pwdev->connected) {
+			/* Already torn down in cfg80211. __cfg80211_disconnected()
+			 * releases the BSSes unconditionally, so a second
+			 * indication releases them again and faults in
+			 * cfg80211_put_bss(). Both an explicit disconnect and the
+			 * netdev_close that follows it land here.
+			 */
+			RTW_INFO(FUNC_ADPT_FMT" skip cfg80211_disconnected, already disconnected\n", FUNC_ADPT_ARG(padapter));
+#endif
 		} else {
 			RTW_INFO(FUNC_ADPT_FMT" call cfg80211_disconnected, reason:%d\n", FUNC_ADPT_ARG(padapter), reason);
 			rtw_cfg80211_disconnected(pwdev, reason, NULL, 0, locally_generated, GFP_ATOMIC);
@@ -11180,7 +11190,12 @@
 
 	#if (LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(3, 11, 0)) || defined(COMPAT_KERNEL_RELEASE)
 	#if (defined(CONFIG_ACK_5_15_LTS_KERNEL) || (LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(5, 19, 2)))
-	if (wdev->valid_links && wdev->links[0].client.current_bss)
+	/* valid_links is the MLO link bitmap and stays 0 for an ordinary
+	 * connection, which link 0 is still used for. Requiring it here meant the
+	 * branch never ran and current_bss was left set on a wdev about to go
+	 * away, unlike the pre-5.19 code this replaced.
+	 */
+	if (wdev->links[0].client.current_bss)
 	#else
 	if (wdev->current_bss)
 	#endif
